# ORIGINAL: TOP1b rows for pieces E and F (the stream wrapper and `set_prolog_IO/3`): `wrapIO`, `Sread_user`, `closeWrappedIO` (pl-file.c:5959-6068),
# `Swrite_stderr`, `Sstderrfunctions`, `getIOStreams` and `set_prolog_IO/3` (pl-file.c:6071-6167), against swipl 10.1.19-1-gcbf4e189 and the C at
# the pin.
# test/core_lang/test_top1b_wrap_swipl.jl — on every term type:
#   * SWIPL-PINNED rows (e01-e12, e16, e17, with e06b/e06c/e04m/e11a-e11e; the pins are generated by evidence/TOP1B/gen_top1b_wrap_rows.py FROM THIS
#     FILE: the helper clauses `_TW_PRELUDE` and the cases `_TW_CASES` are read from the blocks below, so swipl and the kernel run the SAME text, the kernel
#     through term_to_atom/2 and call/1): `open(F,read,In,[bom(false)])`, `set_prolog_IO(In,user_output,user_error)`, then reads of `user_input`. The pin is
#     swipl's WHOLE standard output (the prompt `|: ` that `Sread_user` writes before a buffer fill at the start of a line, and the `<event>` lines the
#     helpers print), two runs byte-identical; the kernel's output is the contents of the buffer that is its `user_output`; ONE extractor (`_tw_enc`) encodes
#     both. No pty: `Sread_user` prompts without the tty test (pl-file.c:5987-6007). Facts measured on swipl 10.1.19: the default `bom(true)` fills the buffer
#     BEFORE the wrap and loses the first prompt (e16, upstream behaviour); a fill that ends in a newline arms the next prompt (e04: a 8192-byte file, two
#     fills); the end-of-file fill prompts again, the next read at end of file does not (the flag set after the fill, e03); `^D` as the only byte of a read is
#     end of file, on a buffered stream only if the file holds that one byte (e06b/e06c) and always on an unbuffered one (e06, `set_stream(In, buffer(false))`:
#     `open/4`'s `buffer(false)` option leaves the file stream `full`).
#   * HAND-WRITTEN rows (registry register/unregister, the `wrappedIO` record, the IOSTREAM `wrapped` field, the `ld_table` template-copy policy, flags and
#     flushes swipl cannot show on a pipe): the expected values are the C at the pin, or the DIVERGES of src/os/pl-stream.jl `wrappedIO`, each testset names them.
#     The kernel's file streams carry no SIO_FILE (src/os/pl-stream.jl `Sopen_julia_io` flags), so the restore of the cleared flag is driven on a stream that
#     is given the flag.
#   * NOT HERE: `Sread_terminal` (piece G: `initIO` -> `ResetTty`), a pty.
# UNVERIFIED on the kernel: gate decides (nothing in this file was run when it was written).
# Mutation-tested by tools/mutants/TOP1B.py.
using Test, LogicKernel
const LK = LogicKernel

